  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near to above average temperatures through the \nweekend and primarily dry conditions.\n\nGOES water vapor channels continue to depict a large upper \nridge over the lower Mississippi Valley. However, it has been \nsuppressed by a trough digging across New England and Atlantic \nCanada. This is pushing a cold front S across the southern Mid- \nAtlantic. Temperatures range from the lower 80s N to around 90F \nS under a partly sunny sky. Isolated to scattered showers and a \nfew tstms are developing this afternoon. The best coverage is \nover the VA Piedmont, with isolated activity over the Eastern \nShore. The front will be slow to push S tonight, so some \nlingering showers are possible across southern VA and interior \nNE NC. Otherwise, partly to occasionally mostly cloudy with lows\nin the mid 60s to lower 70s.\n\nMostly dry with more seasonally temperatures on Saturday behind\nthe front. However, some light showers are possible over the \nlower Eastern Shore and western shore of the Ches. Bay with \nonshore flow of relatively warm water. High temperatures range \nfrom the upper 70s/around 80F at the coast to the lower 80s \ninland. The front likely lifts back to the N as a warm front \nSunday. Still mainly dry Sunday with only slight chc PoPs NW. \nWarmer with high temperatures in the lower 80s at the coast to \nthe mid/upper 80s inland, after morning lows in the mid 60s to \nlower 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A gradual pattern change develops during the \nearly to middle portion of next week with increased rain chances\nand cooler temperatures.\n\nAn anomalous upper ridge builds toward Hudson Bay by Monday and\nbecomes centered in vicinity of Hudson Bay through at least the\nmiddle of next week. Meanwhile, an upper trough slides under \nthe ridge into the Great Lakes region early-mid next week, \nbefore translating toward the East Coast later in the week. At \nthe surface, strong high pressure builds across ON/QB Monday and\nTuesday, with 18/12z ensemble means showing nearly 1035mb. This\nhigh then shifts SE toward Atlantic Canada and northern New \nEngland by midweek and potentially building to nearly 1038mb per\nensemble means.\n\nThe initial change in the pattern will push a cold front S \nthrough the region Monday. Monday will be more of a transitional\nday with high temperatures around 80F N to the upper 80s S. \nThere is a chc of showers/tstms with the frontal passage, with \nthe best chc of tstms S. Onshore flow with increased rain \nchances are expected by Tuesday/Wednesday lingering into \nThursday. The local area is covered by a Marginal ERO Monday, \nand then up to a Slight ERO by Tuesday as an ENE LLJ strengthens\nand combines with increased moisture off the Atlantic. Breezy \nto windy conditions are possible along the coast with \nnortheasterly flow. Not as warm Tuesday, and potentially cooler \nWednesday/Thursday. However, there will be some limitation as to\nhow cool temperatures can get along the coast as SSTs remain \nrather warm.",
